From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 06:03:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: jpeg: decode baseline JPEG on T8132

The engine decodes as well as encodes, but the driver only offered the
encoder. m1n1's JPEG experiment drives a full decode on M1: decode
enable, codec, an output pixel format, macroblock and edge counts,
plane tiling, chroma resampling flags, then a decode MODE. Those
registers map onto the wide64 layout the way the rest of that range
does: the decode block at m1n1 0x130-0x15c sits 0x3c higher, at
0x16c-0x198, which is also the 12-register block the wide64 reset image
already clears.

Add a second mem2mem node per engine, apple-jpeg-dec, sharing the
encoder's job queue so that one job runs at a time. It takes baseline
8-bit three-component JPEG in the canonical 4:4:4, 4:2:2 and 4:2:0
samplings and writes full-range NV12 or NV16 in one buffer. The engine
writes whole MCU rows, so the CAPTURE format is the size rounded up to
16 and the visible picture is the compose rectangle. The engine parses
the tables and the scan itself; the driver reads only the frame header,
and fails a buffer whose JPEG it cannot take (progressive, arithmetic,
greyscale, other sampling factors) so the client can fall back to
software. A new size before CAPTURE buffers exist is adopted with a
source-change event.

The decode MODE is per variant and set only where it was qualified:
0x777 on T8132 (the wide-layout encode MODE without the encoder bit,
plus bits 4-5, as m1n1's decode MODE relates to its encode MODE). On
the J604 both engines decode 4:2:0 (including 1921x1081), 4:2:2 and
4:4:4 (including 641x481) into NV12 and NV16 at 74-80 dB against
libjpeg, a 1080p picture in about 1.3 ms. Other SoCs keep the encoder
only; the decode module parameter hides the decoder node.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
